    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the safety tips. i think i saw a capacitor in there that looked a bit weird, kind of dented. do you think that's what's causing that weird buzzing sound? it almost sounds like something is trying to struggle or something. like it's a weird electrical glitch.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That dented capacitor is almost certainly your culprit—the \"struggling\" electrical humming you're describing is the classic symptom of a weak or failed capacitor trying and failing to give the fan motor the kick it needs to start. A healthy capacitor looks like a smooth-topped cylinder (sometimes with a slight dome), so if yours is dented, bulging, or leaking an oily residue, it's toast. **Make sure power is completely off** before touching anything, since capacitors can hold a dangerous charge even when the unit is disconnected—discharge it safely or avoid direct contact. A matched replacement is usually $15–30 at any HVAC supply store or online, and the swap is just three wire terminals on a photo-documented setup, making this one of the most DIY-friendly AC repairs out there.",
      "source": "target",
